Five fixes to the radxa aic8800-usb DKMS driver (5.0+git20260123) for the AIC8800DC on the Luckfox Lyra PLC,
applied to /usr/src in the image chroot by the board file after the radxa-aic8800 extension installed the package:

1. rwnx_platform.c: rwnx_plat_patch_load() appends the chip directory to the module-global firmware path on every
   probe while the path is only reset later in rwnx_cfg80211_init(); the second probe of a device therefore looked for
   .../aic8800DC/aic8800DC/fmacfw_patch_8800dc_u02.bin and failed. Start from the base path before appending.
2. aic8800_fdrv/Makefile: CONFIG_ARP_OFFLOAD=n. With ARP offload the firmware answers ARP for the board itself and the
   kernel never learns its peers' MACs; on this network the board was unreachable from Wi-Fi clients.
3. aic_btusb.c: set HCI_QUIRK_RESET_ON_CLOSE (hci_set_quirk() on kernels that have it). Without it the controller
   keeps its LE state across a close and the next open logs "unexpected event for opcode 0x2002/0x2039".

4. aicwf_usb.c (aic8800_fdrv and aic_load_fw): the probe stored its bus pointer with dev_set_drvdata() on the
   usb_device instead of on its own usb_interface. On mainline the usb_device is bound to the onboard-usb-dev
   usb_device_driver, whose drvdata this overwrote; the next disconnect of the module (power-off through its
   regulator, or a failed re-probe resetting the chip) then oopsed in onboard_dev_usbdev_disconnect() ->
   sysfs_remove_link() inside the hub worker, which died holding the kernfs lock and froze every sysfs access.
   Point the driver's device at the interface, which all its dev_get_drvdata() users read through the same pointer.
   That slot is the one usb_set_intfdata() uses, so disconnect/suspend/resume now reach the aic_usb_dev through the
   bus (bus_if->bus_priv.usb) instead of usb_get_intfdata().

5. aic_init_fs.h (new, in all three modules) + an #include in the five files that call filp_open(): Linux 7.x roots
   kernel threads in an empty nullfs and path lookups from them must run under scoped_with_init_fs(). The driver
   opens its firmware with filp_open(), so every probe from the USB hub worker (module power cycle through
   onboard-usb-dev, USB reset, reset-resume) failed with "fmacfw_patch_8800dc_u02.bin file failed to open" (-ENOENT);
   only the probe from modprobe at boot worked. The header wraps filp_open() in scoped_with_init_fs() where the
   kernel defines it and is a no-op on older kernels.

